{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-946dc84a2dd5f867","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014 Graduate Llama Stack to GA\n11\t\n12\tStrategy: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability (priority: Critical).\n13\tAll three affected components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard) have\n14\tarchitecture-context files in `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`\n15\tand canonical Jira components, so no onboarding chain is generated. All are\n16\tKonflux-onboarded per those files.\n17\t\n18\tNo Staff Engineer Input section is present in the strategy.\n19\t\n20\t## Epic List\n21\t\n22\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n23\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n24\t| E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n25\t| E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantees and GA hardening | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n26\t| E003 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n27\t| E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as a supported Llama Stack integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n28\t| E005 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n29\t| E006 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n30\t\n31\tTeam assignments: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table (see Health\n32\tWarnings); teams are taken from the owning teams in the architecture-context\n33\tfiles (Llama Stack → RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI → TrustyAI, Dashboard → RHOAI\n34\tDashboard).\n35\t\n36\tComponent mapping notes: E004's work spans Llama Stack and TrustyAI (the\n37\tguardrails integration touches both surfaces); it is assigned the single\n38\tmost-relevant canonical component `TrustyAI` because the TrustyAI team owns\n39\tthe guardrails services being promoted to a supported integration. E006 is\n40\tassigned `Llama Stack` (not the canonical `Documentation` component) because\n41\tthe docs being uplifted are the Llama Stack GA docs and migration notes; the\n42\tRHOAI GenAI team that owns the component also owns its doc content.\n43\t\n44\t## Dependency DAG\n45\t\n46\t```mermaid\n47\tgraph TD\n48\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Inv)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability + GA hardening\"]\n49\t    E001 --> E003[\"E003 Production support posture\"]\n50\t    E005[\"E005 Dashboard default-on\"] --> E006[\"E006 GA docs uplift\"]\n51\t    E002 --> E004[\"E004 TrustyAI guardrails supported\"]\n52\t    E003 --> E004\n53\t    E002 --> E006\n54\t    E003 --> E006\n55\t    E004 --> E006\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\tRoots: E001 and E005. Critical path: E001 → E002 → E004 → E006\n59\t(length 4 epics).\n60\t\n61\t## DAG Justification\n62\t\n63\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n64\t|------|------|-----------|\n65\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The strategy's open question states the GA scope — and therefore the size of the stability and hardening work — is unknown until the assessment lands. True gate: `rewrite` (the epic exists regardless, its scope changes). Recorded via `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` on E002. |\n66\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The supported configuration matrix and SLO coverage must describe the ratified GA scope the assessment produces. True gate: `rewrite`. Recorded via `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact` on E003. |\n67\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer serial) | The TrustyAI guardrails integration is a consumer of the Llama Stack API surface; certifying it as a *supported* integration must build against the final versioned GA API surface and deprecation policy, not the TP surface being replaced. |\n68\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 13 (artifact dependency) | E004's scope validates the integration \"including its behavior across the supported configuration matrix\" — the matrix is the support-posture epic's published deliverable (HLR-3). E004 consumes that artifact, so it cannot complete before the matrix is published. This is a document artifact dependency, not a build-pipeline output consumed as content (which would be an AC gate). |\n69\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | docs-authoring epic blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n70\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs must reflect the published support posture (SLOs, backport policy, config matrix). |\n71\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs must describe the guardrails integration as supported. |\n72\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Docs must reflect the default-on Dashboard experience. |\n73\t\n74\tNon-edges (deliberate):\n75\t\n76\t- **E005 has no dependencies.** The Dashboard default-on change is a\n77\t  self-contained feature-flag/entitlement change in the Dashboard repo\n78\t  (Rule 14: different repo, no shared artifacts). Its timing relative to the\n79\t  GA declaration is release-train coordination (see Health Warnings), not a\n80\t  build dependency.\n81\t- **E004 is not gated by E001.** The assessment's stated scope (API\n82\t  stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling) does not include\n83\t  guardrails; HLR-4 defines the work independently of the gap inventory.\n84\t- **E002 ∥ E003.** Same component and team, but different work-product types\n85\t  (code hardening vs. support-policy authoring) — separate epics per the\n86\t  work-product boundary rule, with no edge between them.\n87\t- No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics are\n88\t  generated: all components are in the platform and Konflux-onboarded per\n89\t  architecture context. Rule 25 is still honored as an AC (\"build pipeline\n90\t  green\") on the code epics E002, E004, E005.\n91\t\n92\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n93\t\n94\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n95\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n96\t| HLR-1 (GA-readiness assessment producing definitive GA scope) | P0 | E001 | Investigation epic; its output gates E002 and E003. |\n97\t| HLR-2 (API stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path) | P0 | E002 | **Bundled**: the remediation of assessment-identified quality gaps (scale limits, error handling) rides in E002 alongside HLR-2. This is not a separately schedulable deliverable — the gap remediation edits the same API/quality surface of the same component and team as the stability work, so splitting would double-touch the same code and create two epics building the same surface. The assessment inventory is an input to E002, not an HLR of its own. |\n98\t| HLR-3 (production support posture: SLOs, backport policy, config matrix) | P0 | E003 | Support-engineering deliverables; kept as a general Implementation (not `docs-authoring`) because SLO targets, backport policy, and the supported configuration matrix are support/product-process artifacts, not Red Hat docs-content-process topics. |\n99\t| HLR-4 (TrustyAI guardrails as supported, documented integration) | P1 | E004 | P1 does not push upstream: E002 is already P0 via HLR-2. |\n100\t| HLR-5 (Dashboard Llama Stack management default-on for entitled clusters) | P1 | E005 | Independent work stream, parallel start. |\n101\t| HLR-6 (documentation uplift to GA standards incl. TP migration notes) | P2 | E006 | `docs-authoring` priority exception: strategy frontmatter priority is Critical → epic priority P0. The P2 label on the HLR reads as relative value; the epic's P0 reflects the docs criticality rule, and the epic remains last in the DAG. |\n102\t\n103\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. No redundant/overlapping\n104\tHLR pairs were found (each HLR names a distinct deliverable surface).\n105\t\n106\t## Health Warnings\n107\t\n108\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n109\t  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria,\n110\t  and no Non-Functional Requirements. Team assignments were derived from\n111\t  architecture-context owning teams; epic ACs were derived from HLR text.\n112\t  Human verification of team assignments recommended.\n113\t- **E002 expected to exceed one sprint.** API versioning + deprecation policy\n114\t  + upgrade-path testing + assessment-driven gap remediation is likely more\n115\t  than a sprint of effort, but it is kept as one epic because it is a single\n116\t  hardening pass over one component's API/quality surface — a split would\n117\t  fragment the same audit. **Re-split trigger**: if the GA-readiness\n118\t  assessment's gap inventory exceeds the estimated remediation volume, split\n119\t  the remediation items out of the stability work into follow-on hardening\n120\t  epics rather than growing E002 unboundedly.\n121\t- **Coordination risk (not a DAG edge)**: E005 (Dashboard default-on) can\n122\t  complete early, but shipping default-on Llama Stack management to entitled\n123\t  clusters before the GA declaration contradicts the strategy's intent —\n124\t  release-train timing must be coordinated, and the epic includes an AC that\n125\t  the enablement is gated on cluster entitlement.\n126\t- **E006 priority reads P0 while its HLR is P2** — this is the\n127\t  `docs-authoring` priority exception (strategy priority Critical → P0),\n128\t  not a priority inversion. Flagged here because it looks like an inversion\n129\t  on a naive read of the HLR table.\n130\t- **Out-of-scope boundary respected**: \"new Llama Stack feature development\n131\t  beyond GA-bar quality work\" is out of scope; E002's remediation scope is\n132\t  bounded to GA-bar quality gaps identified by the assessment.\n133\t\n134\t## Tiered Delivery\n135\t\n136\tNot applicable. There are no external dependencies with uncertain timing —\n137\tall three components are in the platform, and the strategy names no upstream\n138\tor vendor coordination. The single open question (GA scope size) is handled\n139\tby the E001 investigation gate, not by tiering.\n140\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-83473cf1cf19135a","agent_id":"a0e56b6a1c5c0aca8","session_id":"bc08d67b-4a0e-4de8-9000-b947c2646aca","uuid":"6d8e6448-00c0-472e-91ee-1d59ef7c2a85","timestamp":"2026-10-23T19:48:56.505Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-946dc84a2dd5f867","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
